sections.statement:
In five years a whale eats carbohydrates coming from

$$
5\times365\times75\times10=1.4\times10^6\ \mathrm{kg}
$$

of krill. This krill needs $1.4\times10^7\ \mathrm{kg}$ of carbohydrates from diatoms. For $180\ \mathrm g$ of $\mathrm{C_6H_{12}O_6}$, $6\times44\ \mathrm g$ of $\mathrm{CO_2}$ is necessary. Hence

$$
1.4\times10^7\times\frac{264}{180}
=2.00\times10^7\ \mathrm{kg}
$$

of $\mathrm{CO_2}$ is needed, that is $1.0\times10^{10}\ \mathrm{dm^3}$ of $\mathrm{CO_2}$.

The total flow in two years is

$$
2\ \mathrm{yr}\times365\ \mathrm{days\,yr^{-1}}
\times24\ \mathrm{h\,day^{-1}}\times60\ \mathrm{min\,h^{-1}}
\times20.0\ \mathrm{dm^3\,min^{-1}}
=2.10\times10^7\ \mathrm{dm^3}.
$$

$$
n(\mathrm{Fe^{2+}})=2.10\times10^7\ \mathrm{dm^3}
\times8.35\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}
=1.76\times10^5\ \mathrm{mol},
$$

$$
n(\mathrm{Fe^{3+}})=0.75\times1.76\times10^5
=1.32\times10^5\ \mathrm{mol},
$$

$$
m(\mathrm{Fe_2O_3})=\frac12\times1.32\times10^5\ \mathrm{mol}
\times159.7\ \mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}
=1.05\times10^7\ \mathrm g.
$$

$$
\begin{array}{ll}
\mathrm{N_2(g)}+2\mathrm{O_2(g)}\longrightarrow\mathrm{N_2O_4(g)}
&\Delta G^\circ=98.28\ \mathrm{kJ},\\
\mathrm{N_2(g)}+2\mathrm{O_2(g)}\longrightarrow2\mathrm{NO_2(g)}
&\Delta G^\circ=2(51.84)=103.68\ \mathrm{kJ}.
\end{array}
$$

Therefore $\mathrm{N_2O_4(g)}\longrightarrow2\mathrm{NO_2(g)}$ has $\Delta G^\circ=5.4\ \mathrm{kJ}$.

Let $x$ be the decomposed fraction, $P_T$ the total pressure and $X$ the corresponding mole fraction. The key states $\Delta G^\circ=-RT\ln K$ and prints the following exponential without a temperature in its denominator:

$$
K=e^{-5.4\ \mathrm{kJ}/(8.314\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}})}.
$$

It then gives

$$
K_p=\frac{P_{\mathrm{NO_2}}^2}{P_{\mathrm{N_2O_4}}}
=\frac{(P_T X_{\mathrm{NO_2}})^2}{P_T X_{\mathrm{N_2O_4}}}
=\frac{\left(\frac{2x}{1+x}\right)^2}
{\frac{1-x}{1+x}}
=\frac{4x^2}{1-x^2}=0.113,
$$

whence $x=0.166$.

$$
5.30\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}\times35.5\ \mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}
=0.0188\ \mathrm{g\,dm^{-3}}
=18.8\ \mathrm{mg\,dm^{-3}}.
$$

This is sufficient for normal oyster growth.

$$
n(\mathrm{Ag^+})_{\mathrm{added}}
=0.05\ \mathrm{dm^3}\times0.00129\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}
=6.45\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{mol}.
$$

The key's next substitution prints $0.0141\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$:

$$
n(\mathrm{Ag^+})_{\mathrm{left}}
=0.02746\ \mathrm{dm^3}\times0.0141\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}
=3.87\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{mol}.
$$

$$
n(\mathrm{Cl^-})=(6.45-3.87)\times10^{-5}
=2.58\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{mol},
$$

$$[\mathrm{Cl^-}]=\frac{2.58\times10^{-5}}{0.050}=5.16\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}.$$

parts.statement:
Give the predominant nitrogen species:

i) in oxygen-rich lakes at $\mathrm{pH}\approx6$;

ii) in highly oxygen-depleted lakes strongly contaminated by acid rain, at $\mathrm{pH}\approx3$.

Give the predominant manganese species:

i) in oxygen-rich lakes at $\mathrm{pH}\approx6$;

ii) in highly oxygen-depleted lakes strongly contaminated by bases, at $\mathrm{pH}\approx12$.

According to the diagrams, is it dangerous to grind together:

i) potassium nitrate and manganese metal;

ii) potassium nitrate and manganese dioxide?

Give the product structures, showing relevant geometric isomers, for all six reactions of the drawn alkene:

i) $\mathrm{Br_2/CCl_4}$;

ii) $\mathrm{KMnO_4,OH^-}$, then $\mathrm{H_3O^+}$;

iii) HBr;

iv) HBr with peroxide;

v) $\mathrm{BH_3}$ in ether, then $\mathrm{H_2O_2,OH^-}$;

vi) $\mathrm{H_2/Pt}$.

